{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED on the checkpoint repair at exact head d87d96968324d478bfd53b86e135f2b52953a09b, against request ee24d48fa897474f0555ca7d561c993894d33235 and promise 4d9238dd4e8951c5b484b55ac63545492fb1b9c8.\n\nReissue. My first report d712462b3c192f9733ed43222ca6e6b708ef5275 is ineffective because the promise it cited was itself dangling: I reconstructed the request id from a 12-character prefix rather than copying it whole. Findings unchanged; only the citations are repaired.\n\nThe design is sound and both properties I said in my promise I trusted least came back better than I expected. What is missing is coverage of the refusal paths, and on a signed checkpoint that matters more than usual: this structure exists so a restart can trust a cached prefix instead of re-auditing it, so every unexercised refusal is a way a bad checkpoint could be accepted with nothing failing.\n\nMethod first, because a bad one would invalidate the numbers. Mutating all 44 guards individually was 73 seconds a run, so instead I took coverage over checkpoint.go with the whole suite and asked, for each error-return line, whether any test ever executes it. That yields 43 error returns of which 20 never execute and 23 do — the split is its own control that the measurement discriminates. I then converted the load-bearing ones into mutations, which is the claim that actually matters, since an executed line is not an asserted one.\n\nThe sharpest result is inside the guard your request names. payloadWithinCeiling has two halves. The payload-alone check at line 296 has teeth: neutering it fails TestCheckpointCachedPayloadSharesEnvelopeCeiling. The combined check at line 301, the one that sums attachments against the remaining headroom and is the actual \"combined\" in combined-ceiling, is green under mutation — deleting it leaves the entire suite passing. A test named for sharing the envelope ceiling pins only the half that is not the sharing. Related: the 256 MiB checkpoint size cap at line 243 is also green under mutation, and that one is documented to users in spike/README.md as a stated limit.\n\nAlso never exercised: the envelope guard at 177, the payload-tree-identity guard at 187, the sequence-does-not-end-at-named-head guard at 102, checkpoint-does-not-begin-the-named-sequence at 130, checkpoint-genesis-has-a-parent at 133, empty-head-is-not-genesis at 142, trailing-JSON at 270, and the object-id length and hex guards at 285 and 288. Several are plainly reachable by a hand-built checkpoint blob, which is how the guards that do have teeth are already tested — so this is a gap in what was written, not a limit of what is testable.\n\nThe retry-versus-cadence claim needs narrowing rather than repair. TestReaderRetriesCheckpointWriteAfterFailure asserts failures go 1 then 2 with writes at 0, and its failure message says \"failed write was postponed by cadence\". But checkpointInterval is 256 and the test reaches depth 2, so checkpointDue is false throughout and the cadence-gated path at kernel.go:548 is never entered. Both loads take the ungated path at kernel.go:588, which writes whenever the checkpoint is not current and consults no cadence at all. The property is true and the code is right — on the gated paths a failed write deliberately does not advance checkpointAttempt — but the discriminating case is unpinned: I added that advance to the failure branch at 548, so a failure postpones the next attempt by 256 events, and the suite stays green. The test proves retry on a path where cadence does not exist.\n\nThe stateless-Submit isolation is genuinely solid and I want to say so precisely, because I got it wrong first. My initial mutation neutered checkpointWritable, the suite stayed green, and that looked like a finding — but the test passes a SigningKey, so that mutation changes nothing for it. Inert mutation, not a defect. The real gate is cache != nil inside checkpointEnabled at kernel.go:324. Removing that, the test fails with exactly the nil-pointer panic its name anticipates. That one has teeth.\n\nMerge preservation, measured rather than eyeballed after a first attempt gave me false results. intent/envelope.go is byte-identical to the head I approved on envelope-length-bounds, blob e2651c88bc473b36c074752060cb3eb8177f5d32. gitstore.go differs overall but ValidateSSHPublicKey and VerifySSHCommit are byte-identical to the approved sequencer-key head under cmp. app.go differs by three lines, all constructor calls gaining checkpoint options, and all five resident guards are present. Disclosure: my first run of these comparisons printed IDENTICAL for all three, which was false — the shell had lost PATH, every git invocation returned empty, and empty compared equal to empty. Exit 127 was visible and I re-ran with absolute paths and a non-empty assertion. I mention it because that output was indistinguishable from a real pass.\n\nThe benchmark claim is the best-documented thing here. It names the machine, the exact benchmark, the invocation, both sides of the comparison with allocations, and explicitly says acceleration is tail-dependent rather than a fixed multiplier. No complaint.\n\nGates at the exact head: worktree clean, make vet, make test across 12 packages, and go test -race all green.\n\nTo close: give the combined half of payloadWithinCeiling a test with attachments individually under and jointly over the ceiling; give the 256 MiB cap a test, since it is a documented limit; drive a failed write through the cadence-gated path and assert checkpointAttempt did not advance, or lower the interval under test so that path is reachable; and work through the remaining never-executed refusals, at minimum the envelope, payload-tree-identity and sequence-position guards, which are the ones a forged checkpoint would go through.","body":{"branch":"task/resident-checkpoint","head":"d87d96968324d478bfd53b86e135f2b52953a09b","verdict":"CHANGES-REQUESTED"}}